\title{HyperPlonk and the Multilinear Turn}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    Plonkish constraints over the Boolean hypercube rather than a multiplicative subgroup, so the prover never runs an FFT. The clearest statement of what moving to [multilinear](dhsorens-002M) representations does and does not buy.
}

\p{
    To work through:
    \ul{
        \li{The translation of gate and wiring constraints into sumcheck claims.}
        \li{What replaces the quotient polynomial and the low-degree test.}
        \li{Cost profile: no FFT, more commitment work, different memory behaviour.}
        \li{High-degree custom gates, which this handles better than univariate Plonk.}
        \li{Why the field choice matters less here than in the [FRI](dhsorens-0037) world — or whether it does.}
    }
}
